1. Static content

Content that is the same for many users.

Examples:

logo.png
product.jpg
style.css
app.js
videos

For example, if 1,000 users ask for the same logo.png, everyone gets the same image.

So CloudFront can cache it:

User
 ↓
CloudFront
 ↓
Cached logo.png
2. Dynamic requests

Content that changes depending on the user or the action.

Examples:

Login
My Account
My Orders
Add to Cart
Payment

For example:

User A → "Show my orders"

might get:

Order 101
Order 102

while:

User B → "Show my orders"

gets:

Order 201
Order 202

So CloudFront cannot simply give User B User A's cached account information.

It needs to go to the backend:

User
 ↓
CloudFront
 ↓
Backend/Application
 ↓
Database
 ↓
User-specific result
Very simple rule

Static = same content → can be cached by CDN

Dynamic = user-specific/changing content → application processes it


What happens when the cached copy expires?

CloudFront doesn't necessarily keep the cached copy forever.

Suppose the cache lifetime is over.

User
 ↓
CloudFront
 ↓
❌ Cache expired
 ↓
Origin
 ↓
New version
 ↓
CloudFront
 ↓
User

CloudFront can then cache the updated content again.



this is the important part of CDN caching.

Suppose:

Origin Server (USA)
Product image = OLD
        ↓
CloudFront India Edge
Product image = OLD

Now the origin server is updated:

Origin Server (USA)
Product image = NEW

But the Indian CloudFront edge may still have the OLD copy.

So what does the user get?

It depends on the cache configuration / cache lifetime (TTL).

                 Origin USA
                 ┌─────────┐
                 │   NEW   │
                 └────┬────┘
                      │
                      │
              CloudFront India
                 ┌─────────┐
                 │   OLD   │  ← cached
                 └────┬────┘
                      │
                      ▼
                 User gets OLD

The user can continue getting the old cached version until the cache expires or is invalidated.

After cache expires

CloudFront asks the origin for the content again:

User
 ↓
CloudFront India
 ↓
Cache expired
 ↓
Origin USA
 ↓
Gets NEW version
 ↓
CloudFront India caches NEW
 ↓
User gets NEW

Then:

CloudFront India
       │
       │ NEW cached copy
       ▼
 All subsequent users
       ↓
    NEW content
You can also force the update immediately

If you update something important and don't want users to wait for the cache to expire, you can perform a CloudFront invalidation.

Origin
  │
  │ NEW content
  ▼
CloudFront
  │
  │ Remove OLD cached copy
  ▼
Next user request
  │
  ▼
Origin
  │
  ▼
NEW content
  │
  ▼
CloudFront caches NEW copy
Simple real-world example

Amazon changes a product image at the origin:

10:00 AM
Origin = RED SHIRT
CloudFront India = RED SHIRT

10:05 AM
Origin updated = BLUE SHIRT
CloudFront India = RED SHIRT

10:06 AM
User requests image
        ↓
Gets RED SHIRT

If the cached object hasn't expired.

After TTL expires or an invalidation occurs:

User
 ↓
CloudFront
 ↓
Cache expired / invalidated
 ↓
Origin
 ↓
BLUE SHIRT
 ↓
CloudFront caches BLUE SHIRT
 ↓
User gets BLUE SHIRT

So CDN does not automatically mean every edge always has the latest version immediately. The cache can temporarily be stale until its
TTL expires or you invalidate/update the cached object.



What does CloudFront actually store?

This is very important.

CloudFront does not copy your entire application.

For example, suppose your application has:

product1.jpg
product2.jpg
logo.png
style.css
app.js

CloudFront can cache copies of these files.

But something like:

Login
Place order
View my account
Payment
Check my balance

is dynamic and normally has to be processed by your application/backend.

So:

CloudFront
   |
   +--- Cached/static content
   |       ├── images
   |       ├── CSS
   |       ├── JavaScript
   |       └── videos
   |
   +--- Dynamic requests
           ↓
       Application




